<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tefnakht</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tefnakht I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tefnakht was a Libyan chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> who, in the later 8th century BCE, led a coalition of Delta and Middle Egyptian rulers and pushed south. The Kushite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> defeated the coalition around 728 BCE, and Tefnakht submitted by oath without meeting him in person. He is usually regarded as the founder of the Twenty-fourth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tefnakht.jpg" alt="Tefnakht" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilemahos Efthimiadis; derivative work by JMCC1 · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tefnakht_Athens_stela_(T._Efthimiadis)_det.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tefnakht began his career as Great Chief of the West and prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>, and was a late contemporary of Shoshenq V, the last ruler of the 22nd Dynasty. According to Kenneth Kitchen he was the second ruler of Sais, after Osorkon C. He set up two donation stelas in years 36 and 38 of Shoshenq V. The stela of year 38, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buto">Buto</a>, gives him the boastful epithet Great Chief of the entire land and lists his titles as prophet of Neith, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadjet">Edjo</a> and the Lady of Imay. These titles show that he controlled Sais, Buto and Kom el-Hish before the 22nd Dynasty ended, which reflects a political base in the western Delta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tefnakht made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> his capital and allied with other minor kings of the Delta to conquer Middle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, then under the Nubian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a>. He captured and united many Delta cities, which made him more powerful than any of his predecessors in the 22nd or 23rd dynasties. He took Memphis and besieged Herakleopolis, an ally of Piye, and Nimlot, the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis">Hermopolis</a>, defected to his side.</p><p>Two naval engagements checked his coalition's advance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> recaptured Memphis. After further campaigns Tefnakht's allies surrendered and he found himself isolated. He sent a letter formally submitting and swearing loyalty to Piye, but he was the only Lower Egyptian prince who avoided seeing Piye face to face. These events are told on Piye's Great Victory stela, set up on New Year's Day of his twenty-first regnal year. Soon afterwards Piye returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and never came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tefnakht was left alone as prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> and over time restored his control in the Delta. He is generally thought to have proclaimed himself king as Shepsesre Tefnakht I after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> left, and he is thought to have reigned from roughly 726 to 718 BCE. Bakenranef succeeded him at Sais.</p><p>Kitchen and most scholars identify him with the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> donation stela of year 8, although Olivier Perdu has argued for a later Tefnakht II. Tefnakht is absent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>'s tradition, perhaps because the Aegyptiaca survives only in abbreviated form or because he was considered a usurper. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> say he dishonoured the memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menes">Menes</a>, and Diodorus adds that on a campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> he ran out of supplies, lived humbly and came to denounce luxury.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tefnakht” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Imperial expansion is closely associated with Kashta and his successor Piye (also known as Piankhi), who around 728 BCE defeated the Delta coalition led by <b>Tefnakht of Sais</b> and recorded his triumph on a great victory stela at Jebel Barkal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nimlot first acknowledged Kushite overlordship, then joined the coalition of <b>Tefnakht of Sais</b>, which brought on a Kushite siege.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hermopolis">Kingdom of Hermopolis</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tefnakht</b>, Great Chief of the Libu and the Ma at Sais in the western Delta, united much of the Delta and Middle Egypt in the 720s BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-fourth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-fourth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
